.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt EXECVE 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm execve
.Nd replace the current process image
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn execve "const char *path" "char *const argv[]" "char *const envp[]"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn execve
replaces the calling process's image with the program at
.Fa path .
A relative path is resolved from the current working directory.
The loader supports static, little-endian ELF32 executables for i386
with type
.Dv ET_EXEC .
There is no dynamic linker.
.Pp
.Fa argv
and
.Fa envp
are null-terminated arrays of pointers to null-terminated strings.
They become the new program's arguments and environment on a System V
i386 initial stack.
A null pointer for either array supplies an empty vector.
An empty
.Fa argv
is given one empty string, so the new program sees
.Va argc
1 and an empty
.Fa argv Ns [0] ,
as Linux gives it.
.Pp
The limits are Linux's.
The strings of both vectors, the
.Fa path
string as passed, and a 4-byte pointer for each vector entry may take a
quarter of the soft
.Dv RLIMIT_STACK
limit, but no more than 6 MiB and no less than 128 KiB.
An empty
.Fa argv
counts one pointer.
Each string may be at most 131072 bytes, its null terminator included.
The default soft limit of 8 MiB allows 2 MiB.
A vector is read only as far as the pointers fit that budget, and one
longer is refused there; Linux reads a vector to its end first, and
reports
.Er EFAULT
for a pointer it cannot read past the budget.
The initial stack spans as many pages below
.Ad 0xC0000000
as the strings and the pointers need.
The copied path, including any current-directory prefix and its null
terminator, must fit 256 bytes.
.Pp
The kernel checks search permission on ancestor directories and execute
permission on the program.
Even effective user ID 0 requires at least one execute bit on a regular file.
Execution from the synthetic
.Pa /dev
and
.Pa /proc
mounts, or from a mount with
.Dv MS_NOEXEC ,
is refused.
.Ss Interpreter scripts
A file beginning with
.Ql #!
names an interpreter by an absolute path on its first line.
Spaces and tabs before the path are skipped.
The remainder after the interpreter path, with surrounding spaces and
tabs removed, is at most one argument.
The resulting argument vector is:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
interpreter [argument] script original-arguments...
.Ed
.Pp
The original
.Fa argv Ns [0]
is omitted; the supplied script path, made absolute with repeated slashes
and dot components removed, is inserted instead.
The strings added count against the same limits as the caller's.
Linux inserts the script path as the caller passed it.
Only one interpreter level is supported: an interpreter that is itself
a script is refused.
A first line that fills the 128-byte read buffer without a newline is
also refused.
The interpreter is checked for execute permission and loaded as an ELF
executable.
.Ss Credentials and descriptors
For an ELF executable, set-user-ID changes the effective user ID to the
file's owner.
Set-group-ID changes the effective group ID to the file's group only
when group execute is also set.
A mount with
.Dv MS_NOSUID
suppresses both changes, and so does the no_new_privs bit of
.Xr prctl 2 .
Script execution applies neither the script's nor the interpreter's
set-ID bits.
Real IDs are unchanged; saved IDs become the resulting effective IDs.
The initial stack's
.Dv AT_SECURE
value is set when an effective ID differs from its real ID.
Such an exec leaves the process not dumpable and clears its parent-death
signal, as one that changes an effective ID does; any other makes the
process dumpable again.
.Pp
Descriptors marked
.Dv FD_CLOEXEC
are closed once the executable header has been accepted, before the
old address space is freed.
This includes descriptors 0, 1, and 2 if marked.
Other open descriptors remain available to the new image.
.Ss Failure
The path is resolved and its permissions checked first, as Linux opens
the program before it copies the vectors.
The strings are then copied, the path's at the top, then the environment
and then the arguments, each vector from its last string, onto pages
taken for the new stack while the old image still exists.
The executable header is checked, and the pages for the pointers and the
auxiliary vector are taken, before the old image is destroyed.
A failure at any of these stages returns to the caller without replacing
its image, changing its credentials, or closing its marked descriptors.
The pages are given back when the call ends.
.Pp
Program segments are loaded after the old user pages are freed, and the
initial stack's pointers and auxiliary vector are written after them.
A failure then terminates the process; it cannot return to the caller.
This includes malformed program segments, allocation or load failures,
and an image whose address-space regions, the initial stack's pages
among them, exceed
.Dv RLIMIT_AS .
The parent receives a wait status indicating
.Dv SIGKILL .
If the process is the normal system init, its death panics the kernel.
.Pp
The i386 system call number is 11.
EBX contains the path pointer, ECX the argument-vector pointer, and
EDX the environment-vector pointer.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn execve
does not return on success.
A failure before destruction of the old image returns -1 and sets
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/musl/execargmusl.c
executes itself with 5000 arguments and then 5000 environment strings,
which the new image checks one by one; with one string of 131072 bytes;
with strings and pointers that fill the 256 KiB a stack limit of 1 MiB
allows, split across both vectors; and with an empty
.Fa argv ,
for which the new image checks its one empty argument.
It also passes a string one byte longer, the budget one byte over, and
32768 pointers under a 128 KiB budget, and checks that
.Fn execve
returns -1 with
.Va errno
set to
.Er E2BIG
and the caller still running.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ENAMETOOLONG]"
.It Bq Er E2BIG
The pointers, or the strings with them, exceed a quarter of the soft
.Dv RLIMIT_STACK
limit as described above, a string exceeds 131072 bytes, or the strings a
script adds do not fit.
.It Bq Er EACCES
Search or execute permission was denied, the program or interpreter is
on a noexec mount, or its path resolves into a synthetic mount.
.It Bq Er EBUSY
Path resolution entered a mount being mounted or unmounted.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The path, a vector, or a string cannot be read from user memory.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The file system cannot supply a required attribute or read operation.
.It Bq Er EIO
Reading the program or file system metadata failed, or the device was removed.
.It Bq Er EISDIR
Reading the executable failed because the file system reported a directory.
.It Bq Er ELOOP
Path resolution would follow more than 40 symbolic links.
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
The path, its current-directory prefix, a component, or a symbolic-link
expansion exceeds its buffer.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
The path is empty, or a component of the program or interpreter path
does not exist.
.It Bq Er ENOEXEC
The executable header is invalid and no valid interpreter line is present,
the interpreter path is empty or relative, the first line fills the
128-byte buffer without a newline, or the interpreter is not a valid
ELF executable.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The exec workspace, a page of the new stack, or storage needed to resolve
or open the program could not be allocated.
.It Bq Er ENOTDIR
A path component used as a directory is not a directory.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
There is no current process.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr chown 2 ,
.Xr exit 2 ,
.Xr fcntl 2 ,
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr prlimit64 2 ,
.Xr wait 2
